Issue #17 isn't finished. The fix is committed and queued to land, but its build failed, almost certainly because of problems already on main. I've marked the issue blocked instead of closing it, and nothing is tagged.

- **Fix**: commit 4933898 on `looper/doppelganger-17`. It adds an `int32Of` helper that clamps a count to [0, MaxInt32] with explicit checks (no `#nosec`), and uses it at `internal/daemon/panel.go:813`. The new `TestInt32Of` covers 0, 80, MaxInt32, MaxInt32+1 (clamped) and -1. It's the same change as the last run's; I committed it again because the manager stopped that land (exit 143) without merging anything.
- **Build**: the full-gates build of 4933898 (pipeline 3065812242) failed. I can't read its log, so the cause is an inference I couldn't confirm:
  - **tacplus-dev/looper#145 (open)**: every doppelganger build currently fails. The security job scans tacplus's generated code as if it were doppelganger's own and finds 247 gitleaks hits.
  - **#14 (open)**: a reachable vulnerability on main (GO-2026-6859) that also fails the security checks.
  - The other five G115 sites (#15, #16, #18, #19, #20) are still on main, and #20's build failed the same way.
- **Land**: queued with the manager at 18:12Z on the full gate set. The manager will post its verdict on the issue after this run.
- **Checked here**: gofmt is clean, and `looper-trace check` passes with 0 handlers and 0 findings (the change touches no handler). `just gates` and looper-scan have not been run on this commit.
- **Blocked**: the issue comment explains all of this, and the outcome file records `Blocked-by: tacplus-dev/looper#145` and `Blocked-by: #14`. I left out #15–#20: each of them waits on the others, so naming them would make a cycle that never clears.

Once both blockers close, the next run picks up from the land verdict. If a sibling's `int32Of` reaches main first, it rebases and keeps only the call-site change. Then it runs `just gates` on main, tags `v0.1.1` there, and closes the issue. Main has taken no features since v0.1.0 and there are no release branches, so nothing needs backporting.
